From a5b55ae2e4022ee160f87aeb29ebf83d21f7ed65 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 21:03:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): compare macOS worker start times boot-relative,
 not just within 2s [daveey]

b4a68bfe18 widened the worker liveness fingerprint compare to 2s, which covers
the 1.00s kern.boottime re-anchor seen on 2026-10-14. A larger step (long
sleep, manual clock set, big NTP correction) still shifts every live worker's
psutil start time at once and books them all crashed.

On macOS the fingerprint now also stamps kern.boottime: "|<start>@<boot>".
A worker counts as the same process when the absolute start agrees within
tolerance OR the boot-relative start (start - boot) does, so a re-anchor of
any size is not a crash. A recycled PID started after ours died, so both
offsets stay off by at least our worker's lifetime. Rows without the stamp
keep the 2s compare; Linux (boot-relative ticks + boot epoch) is unchanged.

Tests: 30s re-anchor keeps 3 live workers running (no crash, no breaker, no
stale-claim release); recycled PID and dead worker still crash after a
re-anchor; a dispatcher in a fresh process under a new claimer leaves a live
`sleep` worker running with no respawn, and the worker completes its card
with its run id.
